---
/**
 * What this site collects, which today is nothing.
 *
 * Written to derive from the config rather than to assert: each section
 * appears only when this site actually has the thing it describes. Otherwise
 * the page becomes untrue the day one of them is switched on, which is
 * exactly how privacy pages go wrong.
 *
 * Every switch that puts a form on a page has to be counted here. The
 * newsletter is one: it lives on the hub config but renders a signup on every
 * town's events page, so a town with no formspreeId would otherwise go on
 * claiming it has no forms while showing one.
 */
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro';
import { getNetworkHub, getSite } from '@/config';

const site = getSite();
const analytics = Boolean(site.ga4Id);
const submitForm = Boolean(site.formspreeId);
const newsletter = Boolean(getNetworkHub().newsletter);
const forms = submitForm || newsletter;
const quiet = !analytics && !forms;
---
<Page
  title="Privacy"
  eyebrow={site.kind === 'town' ? site.name : 'Inside the Towns'}
  description={`What ${site.siteTitle} collects about the people who read it.`}
  intro={quiet ? 'Short version: nothing. The longer version is below.' : 'What this site collects, why, and how to be rid of it.'}
  narrow
>
  <div class="prose">
    {quiet ? (
      <p>
        {site.siteTitle} sets no cookies, runs no analytics, embeds no trackers and has no forms. Nothing you do here
        is recorded by us, because there is nothing here doing the recording.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>{site.siteTitle} collects as little as it can, and this page says exactly what that is.</p>
    )}

    <h2>Server logs</h2>
    <p>
      The site is static files served by Vercel, which keeps standard request logs — IP address, the page requested,
      the time, the browser’s user-agent string — for a short period, as any web server does. We do not read them to
      build a picture of you and they are not linked to anything else.
    </p>

    {analytics && (
      <Fragment>
        <h2>Analytics</h2>
        <p>
          The site records aggregate page views so we know which guides are read. This is counting, not profiling: it
          is not used to identify anyone and it is not shared or sold.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    )}

    {submitForm && (
      <Fragment>
        <h2>When you write to us</h2>
        <p>
          If you use a form on this site, what you type in it reaches us by email through a form service, and we keep
          it only as long as it takes to act on it. If you submit an event, the event details go on the site; your
          email address does not.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    )}

    {newsletter && (
      <Fragment>
        <h2>If you sign up for the weekly email</h2>
        <p>
          Your address goes to the company that sends the email for us, along with whichever towns you picked. They
          hold it so they can send you the thing you asked for, and for nothing else. We do not sell it, rent it or
          hand it to anyone, and every email has an unsubscribe link that works immediately and for good.
        </p>
        {!submitForm && (
          <p>
            Signing up is the only thing on this site that asks for your address, and you never have to.
          </p>
        )}
      </Fragment>
    )}

    <h2>What we never do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Sell, rent or share an email address. Ever, to anyone, for any amount.</li>
      <li>Run advertising trackers, retargeting pixels or social widgets.</li>
      <li>Ask you to accept cookies, because there are none to accept.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Links out</h2>
    <p>
      These guides link out constantly — to restaurants, town halls, organizers, Zillow, Wikimedia. Once you follow a
      link you are on someone else’s site under someone else’s policy, and we have no say in what they collect.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/contact/">Write to us</a> and ask. If this page ever stops being accurate, that is a bug and we want to
      know about it.
    </p>
  </div>
</Page>
